{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nReview pushes to the production Gerrit (3.14.1) are intermittently rejected with `remote unpack failed: error Missing tree|blob <sha>` although the named object is readable on the host and full fsck passes. The confirmed mechanism (ticket comment of 2026-11-01): when the client's `gerrit/main` is stale, git builds a thin pack whose external delta bases come from another open change's parent; JGit `FullConnectivityChecker.checkObjects:196` accepts an external base only if its reachability walk marked it, so it rejects a readable object as missing. `git push --no-thin` is ignored over HTTPS (Git's remote-curl always runs `send-pack --thin`), so the client-side flag is not a fix. This blocks the ordinary contribution path for every session pushing from the shared bot account.\n\n## Approach\nOperator decision (2026-11-02, final): set Gerrit's `receive.checkReferencedObjectsAreReachable = false` server-side. Gerrit documents this as a `gerrit.config` `receive.*` key (config-gerrit.html, section receive; default true); `jgit.config` holds JGit-native keys such as `receive.autogc`, so the key goes in `infra/compose/gerrit.config` under the existing `[receive]` section (`infra/compose/gerrit.config:60-62`, currently `enableSignedPush = false`), with the file's one-line `#` comment convention. With the check off, JGit still resolves thin-pack bases from the object database and still rejects objects that are truly absent; it only stops requiring that referenced objects be reachable from refs visible to the pusher. Accepted trade-off (Gerrit docs): only disable it if clients are trusted not to forge SHA-1 references to reach hidden commits; every client here is the shared bot account. No client-side packing workaround is adopted.\n\nDeploy path: `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:89` lists `infra/compose/gerrit.config` as detect-only (`config_manual`), so landing the change does not apply it. The documented operator apply is `infra/runbooks/gerrit-outbound-mail.md:62-82`: on the host, `infra/scripts/compose-up.sh` (re-seeds `site/etc/gerrit.config` from the repo at `infra/scripts/compose-up.sh:129`) then `docker compose ... restart gerrit`; receive settings are read at startup, so a restart is required. Before restarting, check for in-flight pushes/submits and do not interrupt one. If that path would do more than re-seed config and restart (image rebuild, data mutation), stop and escalate rather than apply.\n\n## Scope\n- `infra/compose/gerrit.config` — add the key with a one-line comment stating the reason and the trust trade-off.\n- `tests/scripts/test_gerrit_object_store_repair.py` — pin the committed value (`git config --file ... --get receive.checkReferencedObjectsAreReachable` == `false`).\n- `docs/gerrit-aws-setup.md` — add the setting to the server-setting bullets beside `change.submitWholeTopic` (line 280), with the mechanism and trade-off.\n- `infra/runbooks/gerrit-object-store-repair.md` — state that a `Missing` rejection naming an object that is readable on the host is not object-store damage and that this setting addresses it, so the snapshot+repair path is not used for it.\nNo repository guidance currently recommends `--no-thin` or no-delta packing (grep over docs/, CONTRIBUTING.md, AGENTS.md, src/rebar/_guides, infra/), so there is nothing to remove; the docs change records that `--no-thin` is not a fix.\n\n## Testing\n- New unit test pins the committed `gerrit.config` value; run `pytest tests/scripts/test_gerrit_object_store_repair.py tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_signals_gerrit_config.py tests/unit/test_compose_memory_limits.py`.\n- `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass.\n- Live verification on the host after apply (see AC).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `infra/compose/gerrit.config` on merged `main` sets `receive.checkReferencedObjectsAreReachable = false` under `[receive]` with a one-line comment; the new test asserting that value passes, and the change merged through Gerrit with `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`.\n- [ ] `docs/gerrit-aws-setup.md` lists the setting with its reason and accepted trust trade-off, and `infra/runbooks/gerrit-object-store-repair.md` distinguishes readable-object rejections from object-store damage.\n- [ ] The setting is applied on the production host via the documented compose-up + Gerrit restart path, and `git config -f /var/gerrit/site/etc/gerrit.config --get receive.checkReferencedObjectsAreReachable` on the host prints `false` after the restart; the site answers HTTP 200.\n- [ ] After the apply, an ordinary `git push gerrit HEAD:refs/for/main` (no packing flags) of a review change is accepted, and the Gerrit error_log shows no new `FullConnectivityChecker` `MissingObjectException` for it.\n- [ ] After the apply, Gerrit-to-GitHub replication completes and `python -m rebar.mirror_guard --replication --merged-reachability --merged-limit 50` exits 0 with Gerrit and GitHub `main` equal.\n- [ ] The operator decision and its trust trade-off are recorded as a ticket comment.","title":"Disable Gerrit receive reachability check for thin-pack Missing-object rejections"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793642867576534001,"uuid":"a798862c-aa1b-4008-a15c-fe2f21a746f1"}